WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.100
Welcome to The Explainer. You know, if you're building products today, you already know we've got access to more data than literally any generation before us.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:14.620
But as a seasoned product mentor, I see the same reoccurring problem all the time. Modern teams are just absolutely drowning in dashboards.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:20.720
You open up your analytics tool, right? And boom, dozens of charts, hundreds of metrics, flashing green and red numbers everywhere.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:26.200
It feels productive, but without a clear strategy, you aren't actually navigating. You're kind of just floating.

5
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.540
So today, we're going to change that.

6
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.060
We'll walk through the exact framework you need to turn all that noise into clear, actionable

7
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.520
signals.

8
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.660
We've all heard this classic quote, a man without data is just a man with an opinion.

9
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.780
And honestly, it sounds great in a meeting, but I want to push back on that right away.

10
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.000
I got to warn you, a person with hundreds of numbers, but absolutely no structured framework

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.080
is just going to deliver longer, way more confusing presentations.

12
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.440
Having data doesn't automatically mean you make better decisions.

13
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.640
Sometimes it just gives you this dangerous illusion of certainty.

14
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.700
To actually be data-informed, well, we need a playbook.

15
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.600
And here is that playbook.

16
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.720
First up, mapping the pirate funnel.

17
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.800
Two, steering with KPIs and OKRs.

18
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.040
Three, navigating by your North Star.

19
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.380
Four, dodging those deadly data traps.

20
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.420
Five, using leading indicators and countermetrics.

21
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.080
And finally, number six, becoming a truly data-informed product manager.

22
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.190
All right, let's dive right into section one, mapping the pirate funnel.

23
00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:30.270
To really make sense of your user's journey, you kind of have to look at your product like it's a leaky bucket that desperately needs a diagnostic map.

24
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.450
I mean, if you don't know exactly where the water is spilling out, just pouring more water in won't help at all.

25
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.570
That map? It's the famous AARR Pirate Metrics Framework.

26
00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:42.310
It breaks the whole journey into five sequential steps.

27
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.350
First, acquisition. How do users actually find you?

28
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.170
Second, activation. Do they experience that core aha moment the very first time they use it?

29
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.830
Third, retention. Do they keep coming back?

30
00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.270
Fourth, revenue.

31
00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:55.270
Are they paying you?

32
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.730
And fifth, referral.

33
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.410
Do they love it enough to tell others?

34
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.390
The golden rule here is so simple

35
00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.030
but literally ignored all the time.

36
00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.170
You absolutely must fix the biggest leak

37
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.930
before you pour more marketing dollars in.

38
00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.410
Don't try to tackle all five stages at once.

39
00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:09.710
Find your bottleneck,

40
00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.190
focus your entire quarter on it,

41
00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:11.950
and then move on.

42
00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.930
Let's actually ground this with a great case study

43
00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:15.950
from our source material

44
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.870
about a product manager named McCall

45
00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:19.490
over at a company called Velox.

46
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:21.570
So her team was popping champagne,

47
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.790
celebrating 5,000 new sign-ups a month. Huge, right? But McCall mapped out her pirate funnel

48
00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:32.990
and spotted a massive, glaring issue. Their activation rate was only 45%. That means a

49
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:38.670
massive 55% drop-off was happening right after sign-up. More than half of those users never

50
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:44.790
even experienced the product's actual value. So, McCall made a bold call. She froze all new

51
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:49.030
acquisition campaigns and dedicated the entire quarter strictly to fixing that onboarding

52
00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:56.270
experience. Moving on to section two, steering with KPIs and OKRs. Once you've mapped your funnel

53
00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:01.050
and found that leak, how do you manage your team's day-to-day focus? Well, think of an airplane

54
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:06.650
cockpit. In that cockpit, you've got two completely different sets of tools. First, you have your KPIs,

55
00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.630
key performance indicators. Think of these as your ongoing health dials, engine temperature,

56
00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:16.050
fuel levels, altitude. You track them constantly, and as long as everything's in the green,

57
00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.550
you just keep flying. Simple. But your OKRs, objectives, and key results, those are your

58
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:27.450
destination. It's the we need to land in Chicago by Tuesday goal. OKRs are time-bound, super

59
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:33.010
ambitious projects where, honestly, hitting 70% of your target is a massive success. If you're

60
00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:38.290
hitting 100% on your OKRs every single time, you just aren't aiming high enough. So KPIs keep the

61
00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.190
plane from crashing, while OKRs dictate exactly where we're flying this quarter.

62
00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.410
Okay, Section 3, Navigating by the North Star.

63
00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:18.150
North wasn't total signups. It was weekly active teams. Why? Because if entire teams were actively

64
00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:23.130
collaborating every week, real value was being delivered. Everything else, all those KPIs and

65
00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.390
OKRs, ultimately had to serve that one North Star. And, you know, your business model entirely

66
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:32.910
dictates what that North Star should be. If you're running a SaaS company, it might be weekly active

67
00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:37.650
users or tasks completed. But if you're running a marketplace like Airbnb or Uber, you're looking

68
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:42.030
at transactions per week to make sure both the buyers and the sellers are getting value. Media

69
00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:46.610
companies? They usually care about time reading or sessions per week. E-commerce is driven by

70
00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:51.530
purchases per month. The point is, you have to pick the one specific metric that definitively

71
00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:57.370
proves your particular customer is succeeding. Which brings us to Section 4, Avoiding Deadly

72
00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:04.590
Data Traps. Look, even with a perfect North Star, data is exactly like a radar screen. It only shows

73
00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.050
what's in range, which hides incredibly dangerous blind spots just off screen. And one of the

74
00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.910
deadliest blind spots out there is survivorship bias. It's the logical error of only looking at

75
00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.750
the people who actually made it past a certain point. Think of the mathematician Abraham Wald

76
00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:24.750
examining returning World War II bomber plans. The military wanted to put heavy armor on the

77
00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.810
parts of the planes that had the most bullet holes. Makes sense, right? But Wald realized a huge flaw.

78
00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:33.730
The bullet holes they could actually see were on the planes that survived and made it home.

79
00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:37.410
The planes that got hit in the engines, they didn't come back. The most dangerous bullet

80
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:43.610
holes are always the ones you don't see. So let me challenge you directly. Who isn't answering

81
00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:49.270
your surveys? Back at Valox, Michael saw this awesome 40% drop in support tickets on her

82
00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:54.230
dashboard. Her team was thrilled, ready to celebrate, but she dug a little deeper. She

83
00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:59.570
realized that customer churns secretly went up at the exact same time. The users didn't magically

84
00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:04.830
stop having problems. They were just so incredibly frustrated with a new feature that they gave up,

85
00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:10.430
stopped complaining and silently left. The data looked totally fine, but only because she was

86
00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:16.370
accidentally looking at the survivors. To dodge these traps, we need some proven cures. The first

87
00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:21.510
major trap is vanity metrics. You know, things like total downloads that basically only ever go up and

88
00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:26.990
just look fantastic in a slide deck. The cure? Measure actionable usage instead. Ask yourself,

89
00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:32.410
what will I actually do differently based on this number? Trap number two is correlation. That's

90
00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:37.230
assuming Feature X causes retention, when in reality your most active users just happen to

91
00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:42.570
use Feature X. The cure there is hunting down a third hidden variable. And trap number three,

92
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:48.290
the small sample size. The cure is simple, but hard. Patience. Just wait for statistical

93
00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:53.760
significance before making any massive company-wide changes. All right, section five,

94
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.440
leading indicators and countermetrics. Once you've nailed down those actionable metrics,

95
00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.840
you've got to organize them so you can anticipate the future, rather than just scrambling to react

96
00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:08.540
to the past. And that brings us to the crucial difference between leading and lagging indicators.

97
00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:13.180
Lagging indicators reflect past results, things like monthly revenue or quarterly churn.

98
00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.660
Obviously, they're vital for measuring business health, but here's the catch. By the time revenue

99
00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:22.360
actually drops, the damage is already done. It's just too late. That's why a seasoned PM

100
00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:27.060
completely obsesses over predictive, leading indicators like activation rate or weekly

101
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.940
signups. Because if activation drops today, you know for a fact churn is coming next month,

102
00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:37.100
and you actually still have time to fix it. But be careful here. As you push your team to hit those

103
00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:42.780
leading indicators, you are guaranteed to run into Goodhart's law. When a measure becomes a target,

104
00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:48.140
it completely ceases to be a good measure. People will naturally try to cheat the system to hit their

105
00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:53.180
goal. For example, if you strictly measure development velocity, developers will just ship

106
00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:59.140
buggy code faster. That's exactly why you absolutely must use countermetrics. Pair your

107
00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:04.480
velocity with bug rates. Pair customer support response time with customer satisfaction scores.

108
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:10.840
Pair your new signups with day 7 retention. Countermetrics are your safety net. They ensure

109
00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.820
you don't accidentally optimize one single number at the total destruction of your product's quality.

110
00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:21.350
Finally, Section 6. Becoming a Truly Data-Informed Product Manager.

111
00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:26.370
Product expert Peter Yang points out this massive critical difference between being

112
00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:32.230
blindly data-driven and being wisely data-informed. Let's look at one last story from Velox to see

113
00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:37.390
exactly why. So Mikkel rolled out this brand new onboarding flow. After just three days,

114
00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:44.090
on a tiny sample size of 25 users, activation spiked to 71%. Man, the temptation to rush to

115
00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:49.870
the CEO and announce a massive victory was huge. But Mikkel held back. She waited for statistical

116
00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:55.490
significance. Two weeks later, with a solid sample of 600 users, the true activation rate settled at

117
00:08:55.490 --> 00:09:01.470
58%. Now that is still a phenomenal win, but it's much more realistic. By refusing to be blindly

118
00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:05.850
driven by a tiny data spike, she completely preserved her long-term credibility with her

119
00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:10.890
engineering team and the executive board. And really, the biggest psychological enemy of the

120
00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:16.990
data-informed PM is confirmation bias. We work our tails off on a feature for months we desperately

121
00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:22.270
want it to succeed so our brain just magically finds the one chart that says it did to defeat

122
00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:27.870
this you have to build a new habit define exactly what success and failure look like before you

123
00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:33.310
ever open the dashboard write the actual threshold down on paper because once you see those numbers

124
00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:38.270
it's too late your brain will immediately start making excuses for why the feature is actually

125
00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:44.350
working ultimately becoming truly data informed means deeply understanding the limits of your own

126
00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:50.110
tools. Quantitative data, your analytics, your charts, your funnels, it will tell you exactly

127
00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:55.110
what is happening, right down to the decimal point, but it will almost never tell you why.

128
00:09:55.530 --> 00:10:00.430
To understand the why, you have to physically step away from the dashboard and gather qualitative

129
00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:06.150
data. You literally have to talk to your users. Your analytics might clearly show that users drop

130
00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:10.330
off at step three, but a five-minute user interview will tell you it's simply because your checkout

131
00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:15.190
button is hidden behind a pop-up ad. So as we wrap up, I want to leave you with this provocative

132
00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:19.690
question to think about as you audit your own team's goals today. Look at your North Star,

133
00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:24.950
look at your KPIs. If your main dashboard metric suddenly goes up tomorrow, do you actually know

134
00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:29.050
why? Or are you just staring at the dials, crossing your fingers, and hoping for the best?

135
00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:34.130
Data is an incredibly powerful tool, but at the end of the day, it is just a tool. It is entirely

136
00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:38.790
up to you to build the framework, define the strategy, and uncover the real human behavior

137
00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:43.930
behind those numbers. So take this playbook, apply it, and go become truly data-informed.

